{
 "version": "2026-10-03",
 "course": "ROS 2 from zero, in two weeks",
 "modules": [
  {
   "id": "W1",
   "order": 1,
   "short": "Week 1",
   "title": "Week 1: Talk to the robot's computer",
   "description": "The Ubuntu terminal and Python from zero: move around, manage files, install software, and write your first programs.",
   "lessons": [
    {
     "id": "A1",
     "title": "Meet the terminal",
     "minutes": 15,
     "summary": "What the terminal is, how to read the prompt, and your first commands.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Meet **Chiku**, the delivery robot you will program in this course. Chiku's brain is a small Linux computer. Today you learn to talk to it the way robot engineers do: by typing commands."
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "Robots that run ROS 2 almost always use **Ubuntu**, a free version of Linux. On Ubuntu, engineers control the computer by typing short commands in a window called the **terminal**. ROS 2 is used the same way, so the terminal is the first tool you need."
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "The terminal is like chatting with a very obedient assistant. You type an instruction and press Enter. The assistant does exactly that, nothing more and nothing less. It never guesses what you meant, so spelling matters."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Opening the terminal"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "On a real Ubuntu computer, press `Ctrl` + `Alt` + `T`. A window opens with a line like this:"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "student@ros2lab:~$"
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "label": "Reading the prompt",
       "items": [
        [
         "student",
         "the user name (who is logged in)"
        ],
        [
         "ros2lab",
         "the computer's name"
        ],
        [
         "~",
         "short for your home folder, `/home/student`"
        ],
        [
         "$",
         "the computer is ready for your command"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Your first commands"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "Type a command after the `$` and press **Enter**. Try these in the practice terminal below:"
      },
      {
       "t": "list",
       "items": [
        "`whoami` prints who you are logged in as.",
        "`pwd` (print working directory) prints the folder you are in now.",
        "`ls` (list) shows what is inside the current folder."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "term",
       "title": "Practice terminal: your first commands",
       "intro": "This is a practice terminal. It behaves like Ubuntu, but nothing you type here can harm anything.",
       "tasks": [
        {
         "do": "Type `whoami` and press Enter.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^whoami$"
         },
         "hint": "Type the letters w h o a m i, then press Enter.",
         "sol": "whoami"
        },
        {
         "do": "Type `pwd` to see which folder you are in.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^pwd$"
         },
         "hint": "pwd stands for print working directory.",
         "sol": "pwd"
        },
        {
         "do": "Type `ls` to list what is inside your home folder.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^ls$"
         },
         "hint": "A small L and a small S.",
         "sol": "ls"
        },
        {
         "do": "Type `ls -a` to also see hidden files (their names start with a dot).",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^ls -a$"
         },
         "hint": "Put a space between ls and -a.",
         "sol": "ls -a"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "Commands are **case-sensitive**: `ls` works, but `LS` or `Ls` does not. Use small letters unless a lesson shows capitals."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "In the prompt `student@ros2lab:~$`, what does `~` mean?",
       "options": [
        "Your home folder, /home/student",
        "The computer's name",
        "An error",
        "The ROS 2 version"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "`~` is a short way of writing your home folder.",
       "hint": "Look at the \"Reading the prompt\" box above."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Which command shows the folder you are in right now?",
       "options": [
        "ls",
        "pwd",
        "whoami",
        "clear"
       ],
       "answer": 1,
       "why": "`pwd` means print working directory.",
       "hint": "Which command name means print working directory?"
      },
      {
       "t": "fill",
       "q": "Type the command that lists what is inside the current folder.",
       "accept": [
        "ls"
       ],
       "why": "`ls` means list.",
       "hint": "Two small letters."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "The terminal is a window where you type commands and press Enter.",
        "`whoami` tells you who you are, `pwd` tells you where you are, and `ls` shows what is there.",
        "Spelling and capital letters matter."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 1,
     "dayTitle": "Meet the terminal"
    },
    {
     "id": "A2",
     "title": "Folders and paths: moving around",
     "minutes": 20,
     "summary": "Move between folders with `cd`, and learn absolute and relative paths.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku must find the file with today's delivery list. Files live in folders inside folders, so first you learn to move around."
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "Your files live inside **folders**, which Linux also calls **directories**. Folders can contain other folders, like rooms inside a building."
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "Think of the computer as a college campus. `/` is the main gate: the top of everything. `/home` is the hostel block, `/home/student` is your room, and `/home/student/Documents` is a cupboard in your room. A **path** is the address that tells the computer how to reach a place."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Two kinds of paths"
      },
      {
       "t": "list",
       "items": [
        "An **absolute path** starts from the main gate `/`. It works from anywhere, for example `/home/student/Documents`.",
        "A **relative path** starts from where you are now. If you are in `/home/student`, then `Documents` points to the same place."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "label": "Special short names",
       "items": [
        [
         "~",
         "your home folder"
        ],
        [
         ".",
         "this folder"
        ],
        [
         "..",
         "the folder one level up (the parent)"
        ],
        [
         "/",
         "the top folder of the whole computer"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Moving with cd"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "cd Documents        # go into Documents\ncd ..               # go up one level\ncd ~                # go back home from anywhere\ncd /opt/ros/jazzy   # jump using an absolute path"
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "Press the **Tab** key after typing the first few letters of a folder name. The terminal completes the name for you and avoids spelling mistakes. On a phone, use the **Tab** button under the practice terminal."
      },
      {
       "t": "term",
       "title": "Practice: walk around the computer",
       "fs": {
        "dirs": [
         "~/robot_ws/src/my_robot"
        ]
       },
       "tasks": [
        {
         "do": "Go into the `robot_ws` folder: `cd robot_ws`",
         "check": {
          "cwd": "~/robot_ws"
         },
         "hint": "Type: cd robot_ws  (try pressing Tab after cd ro)",
         "sol": "cd robot_ws"
        },
        {
         "do": "Go one level deeper, into `src`.",
         "check": {
          "cwd": "~/robot_ws/src"
         },
         "hint": "Type: cd src",
         "sol": "cd src"
        },
        {
         "do": "Check where you are with `pwd`.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^pwd$",
          "cwd": "~/robot_ws/src"
         },
         "hint": "Type: pwd",
         "sol": "pwd"
        },
        {
         "do": "Go up two levels in one command, back home: `cd ../..`",
         "check": {
          "cwd": "~"
         },
         "hint": "Each .. goes up one level. Type: cd ../..",
         "sol": "cd ../.."
        },
        {
         "do": "Jump to the ROS 2 folder with an absolute path: `cd /opt/ros/jazzy`",
         "check": {
          "cwd": "/opt/ros/jazzy"
         },
         "hint": "Type: cd /opt/ros/jazzy",
         "sol": "cd /opt/ros/jazzy"
        },
        {
         "do": "Come back home with `cd ~`",
         "check": {
          "cwd": "~"
         },
         "hint": "Type: cd ~  (cd on its own also works)",
         "sol": "cd ~"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "You are in `/home/student`. Which command takes you into `/home/student/robot_ws/src`?",
       "options": [
        "cd robot_ws/src",
        "cd /robot_ws/src",
        "cd src/robot_ws",
        "cd robot_ws src"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "From your home folder, `robot_ws/src` means: into robot_ws, then into src.",
       "hint": "Start from where you are, and go in the order robot_ws, then src."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Which of these is an **absolute** path?",
       "options": [
        "/home/student/robot_ws",
        "robot_ws/src",
        "../src",
        "./notes.txt"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Absolute paths always start with `/`, the top folder.",
       "hint": "Which one starts from the main gate?"
      },
      {
       "t": "fill",
       "q": "You are in `/home/student/robot_ws/src`. Type one command to go up **one** level.",
       "accept": [
        "cd .."
       ],
       "why": "`cd ..` moves to the parent folder, `/home/student/robot_ws`.",
       "hint": "Two dots mean one level up. Remember the space after cd."
      },
      {
       "t": "warn",
       "text": "`cd..` without a space does not work on Ubuntu. Always put a space after `cd`."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "`cd folder` goes in, `cd ..` goes up, `cd ~` goes home.",
        "Absolute paths start with `/`. Relative paths start from where you are.",
        "Press Tab to complete names."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 1,
     "dayTitle": "Meet the terminal"
    },
    {
     "id": "A3",
     "title": "Making, copying and deleting",
     "minutes": 20,
     "summary": "Create folders and files, then copy, move, rename and delete them safely.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Before Chiku can learn new skills, it needs a tidy workspace: folders for code, notes and backups."
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "Every ROS 2 project lives in a folder called a **workspace**. In this lesson you build one with these commands:"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "mkdir robot_ws             # make one folder\nmkdir -p robot_ws/src      # make folders inside folders (-p = parents)\ntouch notes.txt            # make an empty file\ncp notes.txt copy.txt      # copy a file\ncp -r robot_ws backup_ws   # copy a folder with everything inside (-r)\nmv copy.txt ideas.txt      # rename (or move) a file\nrm ideas.txt               # delete a file\nrm -r backup_ws            # delete a folder and everything inside"
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "`-p`, `-r` and `-a` are **options**. They adjust what a command does, like asking for tea \"with less sugar\": it is still tea, the option changes how it is made."
      },
      {
       "t": "warn",
       "text": "Linux has **no recycle bin**. `rm` deletes immediately and forever. Read the command twice before pressing Enter, especially with `rm -r`."
      },
      {
       "t": "term",
       "title": "Practice: build a ROS 2 workspace",
       "tasks": [
        {
         "do": "Create the folders `ros2_ws/src` in one command: `mkdir -p ros2_ws/src`",
         "check": {
          "dir": "~/ros2_ws/src"
         },
         "hint": "Type: mkdir -p ros2_ws/src",
         "sol": "mkdir -p ros2_ws/src"
        },
        {
         "do": "Go into `ros2_ws`.",
         "check": {
          "cwd": "~/ros2_ws"
         },
         "hint": "Type: cd ros2_ws",
         "sol": "cd ros2_ws"
        },
        {
         "do": "Create an empty file called `README.md`.",
         "check": {
          "file": "~/ros2_ws/README.md"
         },
         "hint": "Type: touch README.md",
         "sol": "touch README.md"
        },
        {
         "do": "List the folder with `ls` to see both items.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^ls$",
          "cwd": "~/ros2_ws"
         },
         "hint": "Type: ls",
         "sol": "ls"
        },
        {
         "do": "Copy the whole `src` folder to `src_backup`.",
         "check": {
          "dir": "~/ros2_ws/src_backup"
         },
         "hint": "Folders need -r. Type: cp -r src src_backup",
         "sol": "cp -r src src_backup"
        },
        {
         "do": "Rename `README.md` to `notes.md`.",
         "check": {
          "file": "~/ros2_ws/notes.md",
          "missing": "~/ros2_ws/README.md"
         },
         "hint": "Type: mv README.md notes.md",
         "sol": "mv README.md notes.md"
        },
        {
         "do": "Delete the `src_backup` folder.",
         "check": {
          "missing": "~/ros2_ws/src_backup",
          "dir": "~/ros2_ws/src"
         },
         "hint": "Type: rm -r src_backup  (check the name first!)",
         "sol": "rm -r src_backup"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Why does `mkdir robot_ws/src` fail when `robot_ws` does not exist yet?",
       "options": [
        "mkdir needs -p to also create the missing parent folder",
        "Folder names cannot contain /",
        "You need sudo",
        "src is a reserved word"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "`-p` tells mkdir to create every missing folder on the path.",
       "hint": "Which option creates parent folders?"
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Which command copies the folder `maps` with everything inside it to `maps_old`?",
       "options": [
        "cp -r maps maps_old",
        "cp maps maps_old",
        "mv -r maps maps_old",
        "copy maps maps_old"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Folders need `cp -r`. `mv` would move the folder instead of copying it.",
       "hint": "Copying a folder needs one extra option."
      },
      {
       "t": "order",
       "q": "Put these commands in order: create a workspace, go into it, make a file there, and check.",
       "items": [
        "mkdir -p lab_ws/src",
        "cd lab_ws",
        "touch notes.txt",
        "ls"
       ],
       "why": "Create the folder first, go into it, create the file, then check with `ls`.",
       "hint": "You cannot go into a folder before it exists."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "`mkdir -p` makes folders (and missing parents). `touch` makes an empty file.",
        "`cp -r` copies folders. `mv` moves or renames.",
        "`rm` deletes forever, so check twice."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 2,
     "dayTitle": "Files, permissions and installing"
    },
    {
     "id": "A4",
     "title": "Reading and editing files",
     "minutes": 20,
     "summary": "Look inside files with `cat`, `head` and `tail`, and edit them with `nano`.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku kept a diary (a log file) of its last trip, and something went wrong near the end. Let's read it."
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "Robots write **log files** that record what happened, and ROS 2 packages are described by small text files. You will often need to look inside a file or change one line."
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "cat notes.txt         # show the whole file\nhead -n 5 log.txt     # show the first 5 lines\ntail -n 5 log.txt     # show the last 5 lines\nless log.txt          # scroll through a long file (press q to quit)\nnano notes.txt        # edit a file"
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "`head` is like reading only the first page of a long notebook, and `tail` is like reading only the last page. Robot logs add new messages at the end, so `tail` shows you the newest news."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Editing with nano"
      },
      {
       "t": "steps",
       "items": [
        "Type `nano filename` and press Enter. The file opens inside the terminal.",
        "Move with the arrow keys and type your changes.",
        "Press `Ctrl` + `O` to save (O for output), then Enter.",
        "Press `Ctrl` + `X` to exit nano."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "In the practice terminal you can also use the **^O Save** and **^X Exit** buttons. The `^` symbol means the Ctrl key."
      },
      {
       "t": "term",
       "title": "Practice: read a robot log and edit a file",
       "fs": {
        "files": {
         "~/robot.log": "boot: ok\nmotors: ok\ncamera: ok\nlidar: ok\nbattery: 86%\nwifi: connected\nmission: start\nobstacle: detected\nmission: paused\nbattery: 84%\n",
         "~/hello.py": "print(\"Hello from the robot\")\n"
        }
       },
       "tasks": [
        {
         "do": "Show the whole log: `cat robot.log`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^cat robot\\.log$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: cat robot.log",
         "sol": "cat robot.log"
        },
        {
         "do": "Show only the first 3 lines: `head -n 3 robot.log`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^head (-n ?3|-3) robot\\.log$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: head -n 3 robot.log",
         "sol": "head -n 3 robot.log"
        },
        {
         "do": "Show only the last 2 lines, the newest messages.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^tail (-n ?2|-2) robot\\.log$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: tail -n 2 robot.log",
         "sol": "tail -n 2 robot.log"
        },
        {
         "do": "Open `hello.py` in nano. Change `robot` to `ROS 2 robot`, save with Ctrl+O, and exit with Ctrl+X.",
         "check": {
          "fileHas": {
           "path": "~/hello.py",
           "text": "ROS 2 robot"
          }
         },
         "hint": "Type: nano hello.py  then edit the text, press the ^O Save button, then ^X Exit.",
         "sol": {
          "nano": "hello.py",
          "replace": [
           "robot",
           "ROS 2 robot"
          ]
         }
        },
        {
         "do": "Check your change: `cat hello.py`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^cat hello\\.py$",
          "fileHas": {
           "path": "~/hello.py",
           "text": "ROS 2 robot"
          }
         },
         "hint": "Type: cat hello.py",
         "sol": "cat hello.py"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "A log file has 500 lines. Which command shows the newest 10 messages at the end?",
       "options": [
        "tail -n 10 robot.log",
        "head -n 10 robot.log",
        "cat -10 robot.log",
        "ls robot.log"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "`tail` shows the end of a file, where new log lines are added.",
       "hint": "New messages are at the end of the file."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "In nano, how do you save the file?",
       "options": [
        "Ctrl + O, then Enter",
        "Ctrl + S",
        "Ctrl + X only",
        "Type save and press Enter"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "`Ctrl` + `O` writes (saves) the file. `Ctrl` + `X` exits.",
       "hint": "O for output."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "`cat` shows a whole file. `head -n N` and `tail -n N` show the start or the end.",
        "`nano file` edits a file: Ctrl+O saves, Ctrl+X exits.",
        "In robot logs, the newest lines are at the end."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 2,
     "dayTitle": "Files, permissions and installing"
    },
    {
     "id": "A5",
     "title": "Permissions and sudo",
     "minutes": 20,
     "summary": "Read rwx permissions, make a program runnable with `chmod +x`, and use `sudo` safely.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku's new program refuses to start: Permission denied! Time to learn who is allowed to do what."
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "Every file has **permissions** that say who may read it, change it or run it. When you see `Permission denied`, permissions are usually the reason."
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "ls -l\n-rw-r--r-- 1 student student   31 Oct  2 10:15 notes.txt\n-rwxr-xr-x 1 student student   64 Oct  2 10:15 move.py\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 student student 4096 Oct  2 10:15 maps"
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "label": "Reading the first column",
       "items": [
        [
         "first letter",
         "`d` means a folder, `-` means a file"
        ],
        [
         "r",
         "read: look inside"
        ],
        [
         "w",
         "write: change it"
        ],
        [
         "x",
         "execute: run it as a program"
        ],
        [
         "three groups",
         "after the first letter: owner (you), group, everyone else"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "Permissions are like keys to a hostel room. `rwxr-xr-x` means the owner can enter, rearrange and use everything, while others may look in and use things but not change them."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Making a program runnable"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "./move.py           # bash: ./move.py: Permission denied\nchmod +x move.py    # add execute (x) permission\n./move.py           # now it runs"
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "sudo: administrator power for one command"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "Some actions change the whole system, such as installing software. They need administrator rights. Put `sudo` in front of the command; Ubuntu asks for **your** password. Nothing appears on screen while you type the password. That is normal."
      },
      {
       "t": "warn",
       "text": "`sudo` means \"I know exactly what I am doing.\" Never put `sudo` in front of a command you do not understand, and never use `sudo rm -r`."
      },
      {
       "t": "term",
       "title": "Practice: fix a Permission denied",
       "fs": {
        "files": {
         "~/move.py": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\nprint(\"Robot moving forward\")\n"
        }
       },
       "tasks": [
        {
         "do": "Look at the permissions with `ls -l`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^ls -l$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: ls -l  (small L)",
         "sol": "ls -l"
        },
        {
         "do": "Try to run the program: `./move.py`. Read the error.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^\\./move\\.py$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: ./move.py",
         "sol": "./move.py"
        },
        {
         "do": "Make it executable: `chmod +x move.py`",
         "check": {
          "exec": "~/move.py"
         },
         "hint": "Type: chmod +x move.py",
         "sol": "chmod +x move.py"
        },
        {
         "do": "Run it again with `./move.py`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^\\./move\\.py$",
          "outputHas": "Robot moving forward"
         },
         "hint": "Type: ./move.py",
         "sol": "./move.py"
        },
        {
         "do": "Try `apt install tree` without sudo, and read the error.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^apt(-get)? install tree$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: apt install tree",
         "sol": "apt install tree"
        },
        {
         "do": "Now install it the right way, with sudo.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^sudo apt(-get)? install tree$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo apt install tree",
         "sol": "sudo apt install tree"
        },
        {
         "do": "Run `tree` to see your folders drawn as a tree.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^tree",
          "ok": true
         },
         "hint": "Type: tree",
         "sol": "tree"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "`ls -l` shows `-rw-r--r--` for `start.py`. Why does `./start.py` say Permission denied?",
       "options": [
        "The owner part rw- has no x, so the file cannot be run",
        "The file is a folder",
        "The file is too big",
        "Programs only run from /opt"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "The execute permission `x` is missing. Fix it with `chmod +x start.py`.",
       "hint": "Which letter means execute?"
      },
      {
       "t": "fill",
       "q": "Type the command that makes `patrol.py` executable.",
       "accept": [
        "chmod +x patrol.py",
        "chmod u+x patrol.py",
        "chmod a+x patrol.py",
        "chmod 755 patrol.py"
       ],
       "why": "`chmod +x` adds the execute permission.",
       "hint": "chmod, then +x, then the file name."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Which of these commands needs `sudo`?",
       "options": [
        "apt install ros-jazzy-turtlesim",
        "ls -l",
        "cd ~",
        "cat notes.txt"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Installing software changes the system, so it needs administrator rights.",
       "hint": "Which one changes the whole computer?"
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "`ls -l` shows permissions: d or -, then rwx for owner, group and others.",
        "`chmod +x file` makes a program runnable. Run it with `./file`.",
        "`sudo` gives administrator power for one command. Use it only when needed."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 2,
     "dayTitle": "Files, permissions and installing"
    },
    {
     "id": "A6",
     "title": "Installing software with apt",
     "minutes": 20,
     "summary": "Refresh the software list, search for packages, and install ROS 2 tools.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku needs a new tool, the turtle simulator. Installing software on Ubuntu works like an app store."
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "On Ubuntu you install software with **apt**, the package manager. It downloads programs from trusted online stores called **repositories**. ROS 2 itself is installed with apt."
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "apt works like the app store on your phone. `apt update` refreshes the store's catalogue but installs nothing. `apt install` downloads and installs an app. `apt search` looks for apps by name."
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "sudo apt update                        # refresh the list of software\napt search turtlesim                   # find packages by name\nsudo apt install ros-jazzy-turtlesim   # install a package"
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "label": "How ROS 2 package names work",
       "items": [
        [
         "ros-jazzy-",
         "apt names of ROS 2 Jazzy packages start like this"
        ],
        [
         "dashes",
         "apt names use dashes: `ros-jazzy-demo-nodes-py`"
        ],
        [
         "underscores",
         "inside ROS 2 the same package is called `demo_nodes_py`"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "On a real computer apt may ask `Do you want to continue? [Y/n]`. Type `y` and press Enter. Adding `-y` at the end answers yes automatically."
      },
      {
       "t": "term",
       "title": "Practice: install turtlesim",
       "bareRos": true,
       "tasks": [
        {
         "do": "Refresh the software list: `sudo apt update`",
         "check": {
          "aptUpdated": true
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo apt update",
         "sol": "sudo apt update"
        },
        {
         "do": "Search for turtlesim: `apt search turtlesim`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^apt search turtlesim"
         },
         "hint": "Type: apt search turtlesim",
         "sol": "apt search turtlesim"
        },
        {
         "do": "Install it: `sudo apt install ros-jazzy-turtlesim`",
         "check": {
          "rosPkg": "turtlesim"
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo apt install ros-jazzy-turtlesim",
         "sol": "sudo apt install ros-jazzy-turtlesim"
        },
        {
         "do": "Load ROS 2 into this terminal: `source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash` (Day 10 explains why).",
         "check": {
          "sourced": true
         },
         "hint": "Type: source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash",
         "sol": "source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash"
        },
        {
         "do": "Start the turtle simulator: `ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node$",
          "outputHas": "Spawning turtle"
         },
         "hint": "Type: ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node",
         "sol": "ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "order",
       "q": "Put the steps to install and run a ROS 2 package in order.",
       "items": [
        "sudo apt update",
        "sudo apt install ros-jazzy-demo-nodes-py",
        "source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash",
        "ros2 run demo_nodes_py talker"
       ],
       "why": "Refresh the list, install, load ROS 2 into the terminal, then run.",
       "hint": "You cannot run something before it is installed and loaded."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "What does `sudo apt update` do?",
       "options": [
        "Refreshes the list of available software; it installs nothing",
        "Installs every ROS 2 package",
        "Updates the Python code in your workspace",
        "Restarts the computer"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "`update` only refreshes the catalogue. `install` actually adds software.",
       "hint": "Think of the app store catalogue."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "`sudo apt install ros-jazzy-demo_nodes_py` says Unable to locate package. Why?",
       "options": [
        "apt names use dashes: ros-jazzy-demo-nodes-py",
        "You must not use sudo",
        "The internet is too fast",
        "Package names must be in capitals"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "apt names use dashes; inside ROS 2 the same package uses underscores.",
       "hint": "Look closely at the symbols between the words."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "`sudo apt update` refreshes, `sudo apt install name` installs, and `apt search word` finds packages.",
        "ROS 2 apt packages look like `ros-jazzy-...` with dashes.",
        "Inside ROS 2, names use underscores, like `demo_nodes_py`."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 2,
     "dayTitle": "Files, permissions and installing"
    },
    {
     "id": "P1",
     "title": "Python 1: the robot's notebook",
     "minutes": 25,
     "summary": "Print messages, store values in variables, tell numbers from text, and ask questions with `input()`.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku keeps a notebook: its name, its battery level, how far it has travelled. In Python, each line of that notebook is a **variable**. Today you write Chiku's first notebook."
      },
      {
       "t": "goal",
       "items": [
        "Print messages with `print()`",
        "Store values in variables",
        "Tell numbers and text apart",
        "Ask the user a question with `input()`"
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Your first line of Python"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "`print()` shows something on the screen. Text goes inside quotes; numbers don't need quotes."
      },
      {
       "t": "py",
       "title": "Try it: press Run",
       "code": "print(\"Hello, I am Chiku!\")\nprint(2 + 3)\nprint(\"2 + 3\")\n"
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "Look at the last two lines: `2 + 3` without quotes is maths (5), but `\"2 + 3\"` in quotes is just text."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Variables: labelled boxes"
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "A variable is a labelled box in Chiku's notebook. `battery = 80` puts 80 into a box labelled battery. Later you can read the box, or put something new in it."
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "name = \"Chiku\"        # text (str)\nbattery = 80          # whole number (int)\nspeed = 0.5           # decimal number (float)\nis_charging = False   # True or False (bool)\nprint(name, battery, speed, is_charging)",
       "out": "Chiku 80 0.5 False"
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "label": "Four kinds of values",
       "items": [
        [
         "int",
         "whole numbers: 80, -3, 0"
        ],
        [
         "float",
         "numbers with a decimal point: 0.5, 3.14"
        ],
        [
         "str",
         "text in quotes: \"Chiku\""
        ],
        [
         "bool",
         "True or False (capital T and F)"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "warn",
       "text": "In Python, `=` means **store**, not \"is equal to\". `battery = battery - 10` means: take the old battery value, subtract 10, and store the result back in the box."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Nice messages with f-strings"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "name = \"Chiku\"\nbattery = 80\nprint(f\"{name} has {battery}% battery\")",
       "out": "Chiku has 80% battery"
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "Put the letter `f` before the quotes, then write variable names inside `{ }`. Python fills in their values."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Asking questions with input()"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "`input()` waits for the user to type something and gives it back as **text**. To do maths with it, turn it into a number with `int()` (whole numbers) or `float()` (decimals)."
      },
      {
       "t": "py",
       "title": "Try it: trip calculator",
       "inputs": [
        "Chiku",
        "80"
       ],
       "goal": "Run it once as it is. Then change the program so that the trip uses **20%** battery instead of 10%. With the answers shown, the last line must be `Chiku will be at 60% after the trip`.",
       "code": "name = input(\"Robot name: \")\nlevel = int(input(\"Battery %: \"))\nprint(f\"{name} will be at {level - 10}% after the trip\")\n",
       "contains": [
        "Chiku will be at 60% after the trip"
       ],
       "retryHint": "Inside the f-string, change level - 10 to level - 20.",
       "solution": "name = input(\"Robot name: \")\nlevel = int(input(\"Battery %: \"))\nprint(f\"{name} will be at {level - 20}% after the trip\")\n"
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "What is the type of `\"80\"` (with quotes)?",
       "options": [
        "str (text)",
        "int (whole number)",
        "float (decimal)",
        "bool"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Anything inside quotes is text, even if it looks like a number.",
       "hint": "Look at the quotes."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "What does this print?",
       "options": [
        "53",
        "8",
        "x3",
        "An error"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Both values are text, so `+` joins them. To add numbers, write `int(x) + 3`.",
       "hint": "Is x a number or text?",
       "code": "x = \"5\"\nprint(x + \"3\")"
      },
      {
       "t": "fill",
       "q": "What does this print?",
       "accept": [
        "35"
       ],
       "why": "50 - 15 = 35 is stored back in battery, then printed.",
       "hint": "Do the subtraction, then print.",
       "code": "battery = 50\nbattery = battery - 15\nprint(battery)"
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "`print()` shows things on the screen; text goes in quotes.",
        "Variables store values: int, float, str, bool.",
        "f-strings fill in values: `f\"{name} has {battery}%\"`.",
        "`input()` gives text; use `int()` or `float()` to get a number."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 3,
     "dayTitle": "Python from zero"
    },
    {
     "id": "P2",
     "title": "Python 2: should the robot stop?",
     "minutes": 30,
     "summary": "Make decisions with `if`, `elif` and `else`, and repeat actions with `for`, `while` and `break`.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku is driving down the corridor. Its distance sensor says how far the nearest wall is. Chiku must **decide**: go, slow down or stop. And it must **repeat** that check again and again."
      },
      {
       "t": "goal",
       "items": [
        "Compare values with `<`, `>` and `==`",
        "Make decisions with `if`, `elif` and `else`",
        "Repeat with `for` and `while`",
        "Stop a loop early with `break`"
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Comparisons give True or False"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "distance = 0.4\nprint(distance < 0.5)    # True\nprint(distance == 1.0)   # False",
       "out": "True\nFalse"
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "label": "Comparison signs",
       "items": [
        [
         "<  >",
         "less than, greater than"
        ],
        [
         "<=  >=",
         "less or equal, greater or equal"
        ],
        [
         "==",
         "is equal to (two equals signs)"
        ],
        [
         "!=",
         "is not equal to"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "if, elif and else"
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "`if` works like a traffic signal at a junction: red means stop, yellow means slow down, green means go. Python checks the signals from top to bottom and follows the **first** one that is True."
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "distance = 0.8\nif distance < 0.5:\n    print(\"STOP\")\nelif distance < 1.0:\n    print(\"SLOW\")\nelse:\n    print(\"GO\")",
       "out": "SLOW"
      },
      {
       "t": "warn",
       "label": "Colons and spaces",
       "text": "Each `if`, `elif` and `else` line ends with a colon `:`. The lines inside are pushed right by 4 spaces. Python uses those spaces to know what belongs inside."
      },
      {
       "t": "py",
       "title": "Your turn: the robot's traffic signal",
       "goal": "Change `distance` so that the program prints `STOP`.",
       "code": "distance = 0.8\nif distance < 0.5:\n    print(\"STOP\")\nelif distance < 1.0:\n    print(\"SLOW\")\nelse:\n    print(\"GO\")\n",
       "expect": "STOP",
       "retryHint": "STOP appears only when distance is less than 0.5. Try distance = 0.3.",
       "solution": "distance = 0.3\nif distance < 0.5:\n    print(\"STOP\")\nelif distance < 1.0:\n    print(\"SLOW\")\nelse:\n    print(\"GO\")\n"
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Repeating with for and while"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "for i in range(3):        # i = 0, then 1, then 2\n    print(\"Beep\", i)\n\ncount = 3\nwhile count > 0:          # repeat while this is True\n    print(count)\n    count = count - 1\nprint(\"Lift-off!\")",
       "out": "Beep 0\nBeep 1\nBeep 2\n3\n2\n1\nLift-off!"
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "`range(3)` gives 0, 1, 2. Python starts counting at 0, so `range(3)` means three times."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Stopping early with break"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "readings = [1.2, 0.9, 0.4, 1.5]\nfor d in readings:\n    if d < 0.5:\n        print(\"Obstacle at\", d)\n        break             # leave the loop now\n    print(\"Clear:\", d)",
       "out": "Clear: 1.2\nClear: 0.9\nObstacle at 0.4"
      },
      {
       "t": "py",
       "title": "Mini-challenge: patrol with a battery check",
       "goal": "Each trip uses 30% battery. Add `break` so Chiku goes home as soon as the battery is below 20, and does not try to reach the Hostel.",
       "code": "battery = 100\nfor place in [\"Lab\", \"Library\", \"Canteen\", \"Hostel\"]:\n    battery = battery - 30\n    print(\"Reached\", place, \"battery\", battery)\n    if battery < 20:\n        print(\"Low battery, going home\")\n        # add break on the next line\n",
       "expect": "Reached Lab battery 70\nReached Library battery 40\nReached Canteen battery 10\nLow battery, going home",
       "retryHint": "Under the print(\"Low battery, going home\") line, add a line with break, indented the same way.",
       "solution": "battery = 100\nfor place in [\"Lab\", \"Library\", \"Canteen\", \"Hostel\"]:\n    battery = battery - 30\n    print(\"Reached\", place, \"battery\", battery)\n    if battery < 20:\n        print(\"Low battery, going home\")\n        break\n"
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Which sign means \"is equal to\" when you compare two values?",
       "options": [
        "==",
        "=",
        "=>",
        "!="
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "`=` stores a value; `==` compares two values.",
       "hint": "Comparing needs two signs."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "How many times does this print Beep?",
       "options": [
        "4",
        "3",
        "5",
        "0"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "`range(4)` gives 0, 1, 2, 3: four numbers.",
       "hint": "Count the numbers range(4) gives.",
       "code": "for i in range(4):\n    print(\"Beep\")"
      },
      {
       "t": "order",
       "q": "Put the lines in order: count down from 3, then print Go!",
       "items": [
        "count = 3",
        "while count > 0:",
        "    print(count)",
        "    count = count - 1",
        "print(\"Go!\")"
       ],
       "why": "Set the start value, loop while it is above zero, print and reduce inside the loop, then print Go! after the loop.",
       "hint": "The indented lines belong inside the loop."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "Comparisons (`<`, `>`, `==`, `!=`) give True or False.",
        "`if` / `elif` / `else` run the first matching choice.",
        "`for i in range(n)` repeats n times; `while` repeats while a test is True.",
        "`break` leaves a loop early."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 4,
     "dayTitle": "Decisions and loops"
    },
    {
     "id": "P3",
     "title": "Python 3: memory and skills",
     "minutes": 30,
     "summary": "Keep many values in lists and dictionaries, and write your own functions.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku must remember a list of places to visit and the readings of its three distance sensors. It also needs reusable **skills**, like checking whether the way is safe."
      },
      {
       "t": "goal",
       "items": [
        "Store many values in a list",
        "Look up values by name in a dictionary",
        "Write and use your own functions"
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Lists: many values in order"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "places = [\"Lab\", \"Library\", \"Canteen\"]\nprint(places[0])       # first item: Lab\nprint(len(places))     # how many: 3\nplaces.append(\"Hostel\")\nfor p in places:\n    print(\"Going to\", p)"
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "A list is like a train: coaches in a fixed order, numbered from 0. `append` adds a new coach at the end, and `len` counts the coaches."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Dictionaries: values with names"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "sensor = {\"front\": 0.8, \"left\": 0.3, \"right\": 1.2}\nprint(sensor[\"left\"])         # 0.3\nsensor[\"front\"] = 0.5         # change a value\nfor side in sensor:\n    print(side, sensor[side])"
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "ROS 2 messages work a lot like dictionaries: a laser scan message has named fields such as `ranges` and `angle_min`."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Functions: reusable skills"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "def is_safe(distance):\n    return distance > 0.5\n\nprint(is_safe(0.8))   # True\nprint(is_safe(0.2))   # False",
       "out": "True\nFalse"
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "items": [
        [
         "def",
         "starts a function"
        ],
        [
         "parameter",
         "the value the function receives, here `distance`"
        ],
        [
         "return",
         "sends the answer back to the line that called the function"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "py",
       "title": "Mini-challenge: find the safest side",
       "goal": "Complete `safest_side` so it returns the side with the **largest** distance. The program must print `right`.",
       "code": "def safest_side(sensor):\n    best = \"front\"\n    for side in sensor:\n        # if this side is bigger than the best so far, remember it\n        pass\n    return best\n\nsensor = {\"front\": 0.8, \"left\": 0.3, \"right\": 1.2}\nprint(safest_side(sensor))\n",
       "expect": "right",
       "retryHint": "Replace pass with: if sensor[side] > sensor[best]: and on the next line, indented, best = side",
       "solution": "def safest_side(sensor):\n    best = \"front\"\n    for side in sensor:\n        if sensor[side] > sensor[best]:\n            best = side\n    return best\n\nsensor = {\"front\": 0.8, \"left\": 0.3, \"right\": 1.2}\nprint(safest_side(sensor))\n"
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "What does this print?",
       "options": [
        "Nova",
        "Bolt",
        "Chiku",
        "An error"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Positions start at 0, so position 2 is the third item.",
       "hint": "Count from 0.",
       "code": "robots = [\"Chiku\", \"Bolt\", \"Nova\"]\nprint(robots[2])"
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "In `sensor = {\"front\": 0.8}`, how do you get the value 0.8?",
       "options": [
        "sensor[\"front\"]",
        "sensor[0]",
        "sensor.front()",
        "front[sensor]"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Dictionaries use the key (the name) in square brackets.",
       "hint": "Use the name, not a number."
      },
      {
       "t": "fill",
       "q": "What does this print?",
       "accept": [
        "12"
       ],
       "why": "double(6) returns 6 * 2 = 12.",
       "hint": "Follow the value 6 into the function.",
       "code": "def double(n):\n    return n * 2\n\nprint(double(6))"
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "label": "Week 1 test",
       "text": "When you finish this lesson, the **Week 1 test** unlocks on your course page. It mixes terminal and Python questions, and every student gets different questions."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "Lists keep values in order: `places[0]`, `len(places)`, `places.append(...)`.",
        "Dictionaries look up values by name: `sensor[\"left\"]`.",
        "`def` creates a function; `return` sends back its answer."
       ]
      }
     ],
