    {
     "id": "B6",
     "title": "Inheritance and super()",
     "minutes": 25,
     "summary": "Build a new class on top of an existing one, which is exactly how every ROS 2 node is written.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku's cousin Raju is a delivery robot with a basket. Raju should get everything Chiku has, plus the basket."
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "Writing every class from zero would be slow. **Inheritance** lets a new class start with everything an existing class already has, and then add more."
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "Car makers build a new model on an existing **platform**: the wheels, engine and brakes come from the platform, and the new model adds its own features. The platform is the **parent class**, and the new model is the **child class**."
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "out": "Pragyan engine started\nPragyan exploring on 6 wheels",
       "text": "class Vehicle:                   # parent class\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def start(self):\n        print(self.name, \"engine started\")\n\nclass Rover(Vehicle):            # child class: a Rover is a Vehicle\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        super().__init__(name)   # run the parent's setup first\n        self.wheels = 6\n\n    def explore(self):\n        print(self.name, \"exploring on\", self.wheels, \"wheels\")\n\nr = Rover(\"Pragyan\")\nr.start()     # inherited from Vehicle\nr.explore()   # Rover's own method"
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "items": [
        [
         "class Rover(Vehicle)",
         "Rover inherits from Vehicle; the class in brackets is the parent"
        ],
        [
         "super().__init__(...)",
         "run the parent's `__init__`, so its setup happens too"
        ],
        [
         "override",
         "a child can write its own version of a parent's method"
        ]
       ]
      },
