    {
     "id": "B7",
     "title": "Callbacks and timers",
     "minutes": 25,
     "summary": "Hand a function over to be called later: the heartbeat of every ROS 2 program.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku must blink its light every second without anyone pressing a button. Timers and callbacks make it happen."
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "In ROS 2 you rarely call your own methods directly. Instead you tell ROS 2: \"**call this method for me** every half second\", or \"when a message arrives\". A function handed over to be called later is a **callback**."
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "It is like giving the college office your phone number and saying \"call me when my certificate is ready.\" You hand over your number; you do not start talking right away. In Python, you hand over the function's name **without brackets**."
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "out": "Waving!",
       "text": "def wave():\n    print(\"Waving!\")\n\nmy_callback = wave      # no brackets: the function itself, not run yet\nmy_callback()           # now it runs"
      },
      {
       "t": "warn",
       "text": "`wave` (no brackets) means the function itself. `wave()` means \"run it **now**\". Writing `self.timer_callback()` inside `create_timer(...)` is a very common beginner mistake."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Timers in ROS 2"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "class Blinker(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('blinker')\n        self.count = 0\n        self.timer = self.create_timer(1.0, self.blink)   # call blink every 1.0 s\n\n    def blink(self):\n        self.count += 1\n        self.get_logger().info(f'Blink {self.count}')"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "`self.create_timer(1.0, self.blink)` means: every 1.0 seconds, call `self.blink`. `rclpy.spin(node)` keeps the program alive so the timer can keep firing. (`self.count += 1` is a short way to write `self.count = self.count + 1`.)"
      },
      {
       "t": "py",
       "title": "A blinking node (the practice simulator runs for 3 seconds)",
       "goal": "Make the timer fire every `0.5` seconds. In 3 seconds it should blink 6 times, so the output contains `Blink 6`.",
       "code": "import rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\n\nclass Blinker(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('blinker')\n        self.count = 0\n        self.timer = self.create_timer(1.0, self.blink)\n\n    def blink(self):\n        self.count += 1\n        self.get_logger().info(f'Blink {self.count}')\n\nrclpy.init()\nnode = Blinker()\nrclpy.spin(node)\nnode.destroy_node()\nrclpy.shutdown()\n",
       "contains": [
        "Blink 6"
       ],
       "retryHint": "Change 1.0 to 0.5 inside create_timer(...).",
       "solution": "import rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\n\nclass Blinker(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('blinker')\n        self.count = 0\n        self.timer = self.create_timer(0.5, self.blink)\n\n    def blink(self):\n        self.count += 1\n        self.get_logger().info(f'Blink {self.count}')\n\nrclpy.init()\nnode = Blinker()\nrclpy.spin(node)\nnode.destroy_node()\nrclpy.shutdown()\n"
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Which line makes ROS 2 call `self.send_status` every 2 seconds?",
       "options": [
        "self.create_timer(2.0, self.send_status)",
        "self.create_timer(2.0, self.send_status())",
        "self.create_timer(self.send_status, 2.0)",
        "self.send_status(2.0)"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "The period comes first, then the method itself, without brackets.",
       "hint": "Two things to check: the order, and the brackets."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "A timer fires every 0.5 seconds. How many times does its callback run in 2 seconds?",
       "options": [
        "4",
        "2",
        "1",
        "0.5"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Two calls every second, for 2 seconds: 4 calls.",
       "hint": "How many half-seconds fit in 2 seconds?"
      },
      {
       "t": "fill",
       "q": "Inside a class, what do you write in the blank of `self.create_timer(1.0, ____)` to use this method as the callback?",
       "code": "def check_battery(self):\n    ...",
       "accept": [
        "self.check_battery"
       ],
       "why": "`self.check_battery` hands over the method without running it.",
       "hint": "self, a dot, the name, and no brackets."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "A callback is a function you hand over to be called later.",
        "Hand it over without brackets: `self.blink`, not `self.blink()`.",
        "`create_timer(period, callback)` calls it again and again, and `rclpy.spin` keeps the program alive."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 7,
     "dayTitle": "Inheritance, callbacks and your first node"
    },
    {
     "id": "B8",
     "title": "Reading a real ROS 2 node",
     "minutes": 30,
     "summary": "Read a real ROS 2 publisher line by line, then add a listener.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Time to read real ROS 2 code. You already know every idea in it."
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "You now know every idea used in a real ROS 2 Python node: classes, `__init__`, `self`, methods, objects inside objects, inheritance, `super()` and callbacks. Here is the minimal publisher from the official ROS 2 Jazzy tutorial (Apache 2.0 licence):"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "python",
       "text": "import rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\n\nclass MinimalPublisher(Node):\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('minimal_publisher')\n        self.publisher_ = self.create_publisher(String, 'topic', 10)\n        timer_period = 0.5  # seconds\n        self.timer = self.create_timer(timer_period, self.timer_callback)\n        self.i = 0\n\n    def timer_callback(self):\n        msg = String()\n        msg.data = 'Hello World: %d' % self.i\n        self.publisher_.publish(msg)\n        self.get_logger().info('Publishing: \"%s\"' % msg.data)\n        self.i += 1\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    minimal_publisher = MinimalPublisher()\n    rclpy.spin(minimal_publisher)\n    minimal_publisher.destroy_node()\n    rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()"
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Line by line"
      },
      {
       "t": "steps",
       "items": [
        "`import rclpy` brings in ROS 2's Python library.",
        "`from std_msgs.msg import String` gets the String message type: a box with one text field called `data`.",
        "`class MinimalPublisher(Node)`: our node **inherits** from Node (Day 7).",
        "`super().__init__('minimal_publisher')` runs Node's setup and names the node.",
        "`self.publisher_ = self.create_publisher(String, 'topic', 10)` stores a publisher **object inside our object** (Day 6). It sends String messages on the topic named `topic`. The 10 is the queue size: how many messages may wait if the network is slow.",
        "`self.create_timer(timer_period, self.timer_callback)` calls `timer_callback` every 0.5 seconds (Day 7). Notice: no brackets.",
        "`self.i = 0` is a counter attribute (Day 6).",
        "`timer_callback` makes a message, fills `msg.data`, publishes it, logs it, and adds 1 to the counter.",
        "`main()` starts ROS 2, builds the node, spins to keep it running, then cleans up and shuts down."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "`'Hello World: %d' % self.i` puts the number `self.i` where `%d` is. Modern Python also writes this as `f'Hello World: {self.i}'`."
      },
      {
       "t": "py",
       "title": "Run the publisher (practice simulator, 3 seconds)",
       "goal": "Change the message so it says `Hello NMAMIT` instead of `Hello World`. The output should contain `Publishing: \"Hello NMAMIT: 5\"`.",
       "code": "import rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\n\nclass MinimalPublisher(Node):\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('minimal_publisher')\n        self.publisher_ = self.create_publisher(String, 'topic', 10)\n        timer_period = 0.5  # seconds\n        self.timer = self.create_timer(timer_period, self.timer_callback)\n        self.i = 0\n\n    def timer_callback(self):\n        msg = String()\n        msg.data = 'Hello World: %d' % self.i\n        self.publisher_.publish(msg)\n        self.get_logger().info('Publishing: \"%s\"' % msg.data)\n        self.i += 1\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    minimal_publisher = MinimalPublisher()\n    rclpy.spin(minimal_publisher)\n    minimal_publisher.destroy_node()\n    rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n",
       "contains": [
        "Publishing: \"Hello NMAMIT: 5\""
       ],
       "retryHint": "Change the text inside msg.data = '...' in timer_callback.",
       "solution": "import rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\n\nclass MinimalPublisher(Node):\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('minimal_publisher')\n        self.publisher_ = self.create_publisher(String, 'topic', 10)\n        timer_period = 0.5  # seconds\n        self.timer = self.create_timer(timer_period, self.timer_callback)\n        self.i = 0\n\n    def timer_callback(self):\n        msg = String()\n        msg.data = 'Hello NMAMIT: %d' % self.i\n        self.publisher_.publish(msg)\n        self.get_logger().info('Publishing: \"%s\"' % msg.data)\n        self.i += 1\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    minimal_publisher = MinimalPublisher()\n    rclpy.spin(minimal_publisher)\n    minimal_publisher.destroy_node()\n    rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Adding a listener"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "A **subscriber** receives messages. Its callback runs once for every message that arrives on its topic. In this playground the talker and the listener run together, so you can see both sides."
      },
      {
       "t": "py",
       "title": "Talker and listener together",
       "goal": "Complete `listener_callback` so it logs `I heard: ` followed by the message text. The output should contain `I heard: Hello World: 2`.",
       "code": "import rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\n\nclass Talker(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('talker')\n        self.publisher_ = self.create_publisher(String, 'chatter', 10)\n        self.timer = self.create_timer(1.0, self.timer_callback)\n        self.i = 0\n\n    def timer_callback(self):\n        msg = String()\n        msg.data = f'Hello World: {self.i}'\n        self.publisher_.publish(msg)\n        self.i += 1\n\n\nclass Listener(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('listener')\n        self.subscription = self.create_subscription(String, 'chatter', self.listener_callback, 10)\n\n    def listener_callback(self, msg):\n        # log the received text here, using msg.data\n        pass\n\n\nrclpy.init()\nlistener = Listener()\ntalker = Talker()\nrclpy.spin(talker)\nrclpy.shutdown()\n",
       "contains": [
        "I heard: Hello World: 2"
       ],
       "retryHint": "Replace pass with self.get_logger().info(f'I heard: {msg.data}')",
       "solution": "import rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\n\nclass Talker(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('talker')\n        self.publisher_ = self.create_publisher(String, 'chatter', 10)\n        self.timer = self.create_timer(1.0, self.timer_callback)\n        self.i = 0\n\n    def timer_callback(self):\n        msg = String()\n        msg.data = f'Hello World: {self.i}'\n        self.publisher_.publish(msg)\n        self.i += 1\n\n\nclass Listener(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('listener')\n        self.subscription = self.create_subscription(String, 'chatter', self.listener_callback, 10)\n\n    def listener_callback(self, msg):\n        self.get_logger().info(f'I heard: {msg.data}')\n\n\nrclpy.init()\nlistener = Listener()\ntalker = Talker()\nrclpy.spin(talker)\nrclpy.shutdown()\n"
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "In `self.create_publisher(String, 'chatter', 10)`, what is `'chatter'`?",
       "options": [
        "The topic name the messages are sent on",
        "The node name",
        "The message text",
        "The queue size"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "The second value is the topic name.",
       "hint": "message type, topic name, queue size."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "When does `listener_callback` run?",
       "options": [
        "Automatically, once for every message that arrives on its topic",
        "Only when you call it yourself",
        "Once, when the node starts",
        "Every 10 seconds"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "ROS 2 calls the subscription's callback for every new message.",
       "hint": "It is a callback."
      },
      {
       "t": "order",
       "q": "Put the lines of `main()` in the correct order.",
       "items": [
        "rclpy.init(args=args)",
        "node = MinimalPublisher()",
        "rclpy.spin(node)",
        "node.destroy_node()",
        "rclpy.shutdown()"
       ],
       "why": "Start ROS 2, build the node, keep it running, then clean up and shut down.",
       "hint": "Start, build, run, clean up, stop."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "A ROS 2 Python node is a class that inherits from `Node`.",
        "Publishers and timers are objects stored with `self.`, and callbacks are passed without brackets.",
        "`main()` runs init, creates the node, spins, then destroys the node and shuts down.",
        "Next step: run these real nodes on Ubuntu in the lab."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 7,
     "dayTitle": "Inheritance, callbacks and your first node"
    },
    {
     "id": "C1",
     "title": "Hello, C++: compile and run",
     "minutes": 30,
     "summary": "Write, compile and run your first C++ program, and fix the most common error.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku's motors need very fast decisions, hundreds every second. For speed-critical parts, robot engineers often use **C++**, and many ROS 2 drivers and controllers are written in it. Good news: the ideas are the same as in Python; only the spelling is stricter."
      },
      {
       "t": "goal",
       "items": [
        "Write, compile and run a C++ program",
        "Print with `std::cout`",
        "Create variables with a type",
        "Fix the most common error: a missing semicolon"
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "Python reads your recipe and cooks line by line as it reads. C++ first translates the **whole** recipe into machine language (this is called **compiling**), and only then cooks. Translating takes a moment, but the cooking is then very fast."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Your first C++ program"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "cpp",
       "text": "#include <iostream>       // lets us use std::cout\n\nint main() {             // every C++ program starts here\n  std::cout << \"Hello, I am Chiku!\" << std::endl;\n  return 0;              // 0 means: finished without problems\n}"
      },
      {
       "t": "steps",
       "items": [
        "Write the code in a file ending in `.cpp`, for example with `nano hello.cpp`.",
        "Compile it: `g++ hello.cpp -o hello`. This makes a program called hello.",
        "Run the program: `./hello`."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "term",
       "title": "Practice: compile and run C++",
       "fs": {
        "files": {
         "~/hello.cpp": "#include <iostream>       // lets us use std::cout\n\nint main() {             // every C++ program starts here\n  std::cout << \"Hello, I am Chiku!\" << std::endl;\n  return 0;              // 0 means: finished without problems\n}\n"
        }
       },
       "tasks": [
        {
         "do": "Look at the code: `cat hello.cpp`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^cat hello\\.cpp$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: cat hello.cpp",
         "sol": "cat hello.cpp"
        },
        {
         "do": "Compile it: `g++ hello.cpp -o hello`",
         "check": {
          "exec": "~/hello"
         },
         "hint": "Type: g++ hello.cpp -o hello",
         "sol": "g++ hello.cpp -o hello"
        },
        {
         "do": "Run your program: `./hello`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^\\./hello$",
          "outputHas": "Hello, I am Chiku!"
         },
         "hint": "Type: ./hello",
         "sol": "./hello"
        },
        {
         "do": "Open `hello.cpp` in nano and change the message to `Hello, ROS 2!`. Save with Ctrl+O and exit with Ctrl+X.",
         "check": {
          "fileHas": {
           "path": "~/hello.cpp",
           "text": "Hello, ROS 2!"
          }
         },
         "hint": "Type: nano hello.cpp, change the text between the quotes, then press the ^O Save and ^X Exit buttons.",
         "sol": {
          "nano": "hello.cpp",
          "replace": [
           "Hello, I am Chiku!",
           "Hello, ROS 2!"
          ]
         }
        },
        {
         "do": "Run `./hello` again. Is the message new? Then compile again with g++ and run once more.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^\\./hello$",
          "outputHas": "Hello, ROS 2!"
         },
         "hint": "The program changes only after you compile again: g++ hello.cpp -o hello, then ./hello",
         "sol": [
          "./hello",
          "g++ hello.cpp -o hello",
          "./hello"
         ]
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "Did you notice? After editing, `./hello` still printed the old message until you compiled again. In C++, you must **compile after every change**."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Variables have a type"
      },
      {
       "t": "twin",
       "left": {
        "label": "Python",
        "code": "name = \"Chiku\"\nwheels = 4\nspeed = 0.5\nis_charging = False\nprint(name, wheels, speed)",
        "lang": "python"
       },
       "right": {
        "label": "C++",
        "code": "std::string name = \"Chiku\";\nint wheels = 4;\ndouble speed = 0.5;\nbool is_charging = false;\nstd::cout << name << \" \" << wheels << \" \" << speed << std::endl;",
        "lang": "cpp"
       }
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "label": "C++ rules that Python doesn't have",
       "items": [
        [
         "type first",
         "write the type before the name: `int wheels = 4;`"
        ],
        [
         ";",
         "every statement ends with a semicolon"
        ],
        [
         "{ }",
         "curly brackets group lines (Python uses indentation)"
        ],
        [
         "std::",
         "the name tag of C++'s standard tools, as in `std::cout` and `std::string`"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "term",
       "title": "Practice: fix the missing semicolon",
       "fs": {
        "files": {
         "~/speed.cpp": "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n  double speed = 0.5;\n  std::cout << \"Speed: \" << speed << std::endl\n  return 0;\n}\n"
        }
       },
       "tasks": [
        {
         "do": "Compile it: `g++ speed.cpp -o speed`. Read the error: which line is it about?",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^g\\+\\+ speed\\.cpp -o speed$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: g++ speed.cpp -o speed",
         "sol": "g++ speed.cpp -o speed"
        },
        {
         "do": "Open `speed.cpp` in nano and add `;` at the end of line 5. Save and exit.",
         "check": {
          "fileHas": {
           "path": "~/speed.cpp",
           "text": "std::endl;"
          }
         },
         "hint": "Type: nano speed.cpp, go to the end of the std::cout line, type ; then save and exit.",
         "sol": {
          "nano": "speed.cpp",
          "replace": [
           "std::endl\n",
           "std::endl;\n"
          ]
         }
        },
        {
         "do": "Compile again, then run `./speed`.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^\\./speed$",
          "outputHas": "Speed: 0.5"
         },
         "hint": "Type: g++ speed.cpp -o speed  then  ./speed",
         "sol": [
          "g++ speed.cpp -o speed",
          "./speed"
         ]
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "What does `g++ robot.cpp -o robot` do?",
       "options": [
        "Translates robot.cpp into a program called robot",
        "Runs robot.cpp line by line",
        "Deletes robot.cpp",
        "Installs the g++ compiler"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "g++ compiles: it translates the source code into a program you can run with ./robot.",
       "hint": "Think of the recipe translation."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Which line is correct C++?",
       "options": [
        "int wheels = 4;",
        "wheels = 4",
        "int wheels = 4",
        "int: wheels = 4;"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Type, name, value, and a semicolon at the end.",
       "hint": "Check the type and the end of the line."
      },
      {
       "t": "fill",
       "q": "What does this C++ line print?",
       "accept": [
        "Battery: 75%"
       ],
       "why": "Each piece after `<<` is printed in order.",
       "hint": "Join the three pieces together.",
       "code": "std::cout << \"Battery: \" << 75 << \"%\" << std::endl;"
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "C++ is compiled: write `file.cpp`, run `g++ file.cpp -o name`, then `./name`.",
        "Compile again after every change.",
        "Every variable has a type: `int`, `double`, `bool`, `std::string`.",
        "Statements end with `;` and blocks use `{ }`."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 8,
     "dayTitle": "C++ basics"
    },
    {
     "id": "C2",
     "title": "C++ decisions, loops, functions and vectors",
     "minutes": 30,
     "summary": "The Python skills you know, in C++: `if`, `for`, functions with types, and `std::vector`.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Chiku's C++ brain needs the same skills you learned in Python: decide, repeat and reuse. Let's put Python and C++ side by side."
      },
      {
       "t": "goal",
       "items": [
        "Write `if`/`else` and `for` loops in C++",
        "Write a C++ function with types",
        "Keep many values in a `std::vector`"
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Decisions"
      },
      {
       "t": "twin",
       "left": {
        "label": "Python",
        "code": "if distance < 0.5:\n    print(\"STOP\")\nelse:\n    print(\"GO\")",
        "lang": "python"
       },
       "right": {
        "label": "C++",
        "code": "if (distance < 0.5) {\n  std::cout << \"STOP\" << std::endl;\n} else {\n  std::cout << \"GO\" << std::endl;\n}",
        "lang": "cpp"
       }
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "In C++ the test goes inside round brackets `( )`, and the lines to run go inside curly brackets `{ }`."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Loops"
      },
      {
       "t": "twin",
       "left": {
        "label": "Python",
        "code": "for i in range(3):\n    print(\"Step\", i)",
        "lang": "python"
       },
       "right": {
        "label": "C++",
        "code": "for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n  std::cout << \"Step \" << i << std::endl;\n}",
        "lang": "cpp"
       }
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "label": "Reading a C++ for loop",
       "items": [
        [
         "int i = 0",
         "start: i begins at 0"
        ],
        [
         "i < 3",
         "keep going while this is true"
        ],
        [
         "i++",
         "after each round, add 1 to i"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "term",
       "title": "Practice: a counting loop in C++",
       "fs": {
        "files": {
         "~/count.cpp": "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n  int steps = 3;\n  for (int i = 1; i <= steps; i++) {\n    std::cout << \"Step \" << i << std::endl;\n  }\n  std::cout << \"Arrived!\" << std::endl;\n  return 0;\n}\n"
        }
       },
       "tasks": [
        {
         "do": "Compile and run it: `g++ count.cpp -o count`, then `./count`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^\\./count$",
          "outputHas": "Arrived!"
         },
         "hint": "Type: g++ count.cpp -o count  then  ./count",
         "sol": [
          "g++ count.cpp -o count",
          "./count"
         ]
        },
        {
         "do": "In nano, change `int steps = 3;` to `int steps = 5;`. Compile and run again: Chiku should take 5 steps.",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^\\./count$",
          "outputHas": "Step 5"
         },
         "hint": "nano count.cpp, change 3 to 5, save, exit, then g++ count.cpp -o count and ./count",
         "sol": [
          {
           "nano": "count.cpp",
           "replace": [
            "int steps = 3;",
            "int steps = 5;"
           ]
          },
          "g++ count.cpp -o count",
          "./count"
         ]
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Functions with types"
      },
      {
       "t": "twin",
       "left": {
        "label": "Python",
        "code": "def is_safe(distance):\n    return distance > 0.5",
        "lang": "python"
       },
       "right": {
        "label": "C++",
        "code": "bool is_safe(double distance) {\n  return distance > 0.5;\n}",
        "lang": "cpp"
       }
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "A C++ function says the **type of its answer** (`bool`) and the **type of each input** (`double distance`)."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Lists in C++: std::vector"
      },
      {
       "t": "twin",
       "left": {
        "label": "Python",
        "code": "readings = [1.2, 0.9, 0.4]\nreadings.append(2.0)\nprint(len(readings))",
        "lang": "python"
       },
       "right": {
        "label": "C++",
        "code": "std::vector<double> readings = {1.2, 0.9, 0.4};\nreadings.push_back(2.0);\nstd::cout << readings.size() << std::endl;",
        "lang": "cpp"
       }
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "`std::vector` is C++'s list (add `#include <vector>` at the top). In ROS 2 C++ code, a laser scanner's distance readings arrive as a `std::vector<float>`."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "How many times does this loop run?",
       "options": [
        "4",
        "3",
        "5",
        "Forever"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "i takes the values 0, 1, 2 and 3.",
       "hint": "Write down the values of i.",
       "code": "for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << std::endl;\n}"
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Which line adds 2.5 to the end of `std::vector<double> d`?",
       "options": [
        "d.push_back(2.5);",
        "d.append(2.5);",
        "d += 2.5;",
        "push(d, 2.5);"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "C++ vectors use `push_back`; Python lists use `append`.",
       "hint": "It is not the Python word."
      },
      {
       "t": "order",
       "q": "Put this C++ program in order.",
       "items": [
        "#include <iostream>",
        "int main() {",
        "  std::cout << \"Hi\" << std::endl;",
        "  return 0;",
        "}"
       ],
       "why": "Includes first, then main with its body inside curly brackets.",
       "hint": "Every program starts with its #include lines."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "`if (test) { ... } else { ... }`",
        "`for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { ... }` repeats n times.",
        "Functions declare types: `bool is_safe(double d)`.",
        "`std::vector` is C++'s list: `push_back`, `size()`."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 8,
     "dayTitle": "C++ basics"
    },
    {
     "id": "C3",
     "title": "C++ classes: same blueprint, stricter rules",
     "minutes": 30,
     "summary": "Write a C++ class with a constructor, member functions, `public` and `private`, and inheritance.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Remember the Python Robot blueprint from Day 6? Today you build the same blueprint in C++. The ideas are the same; the rules are stricter."
      },
      {
       "t": "goal",
       "items": [
        "Read and write a C++ class with a constructor and member functions",
        "Use `public` and `private`",
        "Recognise inheritance in C++"
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "twin",
       "left": {
        "label": "Python",
        "code": "class Robot:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        self.battery = 100\n\n    def move(self, metres):\n        self.battery -= metres * 2\n        print(self.name, \"battery\", self.battery)\n\nr = Robot(\"Chiku\")\nr.move(5)",
        "lang": "python"
       },
       "right": {
        "label": "C++",
        "code": "class Robot {\npublic:\n  Robot(std::string name) : name_(name), battery_(100) {}\n\n  void move(int metres) {\n    battery_ -= metres * 2;\n    std::cout << name_ << \" battery \" << battery_ << std::endl;\n  }\n\nprivate:\n  std::string name_;\n  int battery_;\n};\n\nRobot r(\"Chiku\");\nr.move(5);",
        "lang": "cpp"
       }
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "label": "Python and C++ classes compared",
       "items": [
        [
         "__init__",
         "the **constructor**: a function with the class's name, `Robot(...)`"
        ],
        [
         "self.name",
         "`name_`: C++ code often ends member names with `_`, and doesn't write `self`"
        ],
        [
         ": name_(name)",
         "the initialiser list: sets members before the constructor's body runs"
        ],
        [
         "public: / private:",
         "who may use it: everyone / only the class itself"
        ],
        [
         "};",
         "a C++ class ends with a semicolon"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "analogy",
       "text": "`public` and `private` are like a robot's control panel and its insides. Anyone may press the buttons on the panel (public functions like `move`). Only the robot itself touches its insides (private data like `battery_`), so nobody can set the battery to -50 by mistake."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Inheritance in C++"
      },
      {
       "t": "twin",
       "left": {
        "label": "Python",
        "code": "class DeliveryRobot(Robot):\n    def __init__(self, name, item):\n        super().__init__(name)\n        self.item = item",
        "lang": "python"
       },
       "right": {
        "label": "C++",
        "code": "class DeliveryRobot : public Robot {\npublic:\n  DeliveryRobot(std::string name, std::string item)\n    : Robot(name), item_(item) {}\nprivate:\n  std::string item_;\n};",
        "lang": "cpp"
       }
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "`: public Robot` means DeliveryRobot inherits from Robot. Calling `Robot(name)` in the initialiser list does the job of Python's `super().__init__(name)`."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "In C++, which part does the job of Python's `__init__`?",
       "options": [
        "The constructor: a function with the same name as the class",
        "main()",
        "#include",
        "The semicolon after the class"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "The constructor runs automatically when an object is created, like `__init__`.",
       "hint": "Which function has the class's name?"
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "`battery_` is private. Which line gives a compile error?",
       "options": [
        "r.battery_ = 500;",
        "Robot r(\"Chiku\");",
        "r.move(5);",
        "None of them"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Private members can only be used inside the class's own functions.",
       "hint": "Which line touches the robot's insides from outside?",
       "code": "Robot r(\"Chiku\");\nr.move(5);\nr.battery_ = 500;"
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "What is the parent class in `class Rover : public Robot`?",
       "options": [
        "Robot",
        "Rover",
        "public",
        "class"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "The class after `: public` is the parent.",
       "hint": "Look after the colon."
      },
      {
       "t": "fill",
       "q": "Complete the line so that Rover inherits from Robot: `class Rover : ______ Robot {`",
       "accept": [
        "public"
       ],
       "why": "`class Child : public Parent` is how C++ writes inheritance.",
       "hint": "It is the same word as the control-panel section."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "A C++ class has a constructor (like `__init__`), member functions and member variables.",
        "`public` is the control panel; `private` is the robot's insides.",
        "`class Child : public Parent` inherits; call `Parent(...)` in the initialiser list."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 9,
     "dayTitle": "C++ classes and ROS 2 C++ nodes"
    },
    {
     "id": "C4",
     "title": "Reading a real ROS 2 C++ node",
     "minutes": 30,
     "summary": "Find the class, constructor, publisher, timer and callback inside the official C++ publisher.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "You can already read a Python ROS 2 node. A C++ node has exactly the same parts. Today you find each Python idea inside a real C++ node."
      },
      {
       "t": "goal",
       "items": [
        "Find the class, constructor, publisher, timer and callback in an rclcpp node",
        "Read `std::make_shared` and lambdas without fear",
        "Match each C++ line with its Python twin"
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "This is the minimal publisher from the official ROS 2 Jazzy C++ tutorial (Apache 2.0 licence). It sends a message twice per second."
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "cpp",
       "text": "#include <chrono>\n#include <memory>\n#include <string>\n\n#include \"rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp\"\n#include \"std_msgs/msg/string.hpp\"\n\nusing namespace std::chrono_literals;\n\nclass MinimalPublisher : public rclcpp::Node\n{\npublic:\n  MinimalPublisher()\n  : Node(\"minimal_publisher\"), count_(0)\n  {\n    publisher_ = this->create_publisher<std_msgs::msg::String>(\"topic\", 10);\n    auto timer_callback =\n      [this]() -> void {\n        auto message = std_msgs::msg::String();\n        message.data = \"Hello, world! \" + std::to_string(this->count_++);\n        RCLCPP_INFO(this->get_logger(), \"Publishing: '%s'\", message.data.c_str());\n        this->publisher_->publish(message);\n      };\n    timer_ = this->create_wall_timer(500ms, timer_callback);\n  }\n\nprivate:\n  rclcpp::TimerBase::SharedPtr timer_;\n  rclcpp::Publisher<std_msgs::msg::String>::SharedPtr publisher_;\n  size_t count_;\n};\n\nint main(int argc, char * argv[])\n{\n  rclcpp::init(argc, argv);\n  rclcpp::spin(std::make_shared<MinimalPublisher>());\n  rclcpp::shutdown();\n  return 0;\n}"
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Line by line"
      },
      {
       "t": "steps",
       "items": [
        "`class MinimalPublisher : public rclcpp::Node` inherits from Node, like Python's `class MinimalPublisher(Node)`.",
        "`MinimalPublisher() : Node(\"minimal_publisher\"), count_(0)` is the constructor. It names the node, like `super().__init__('minimal_publisher')`, and sets the counter to 0.",
        "`create_publisher<std_msgs::msg::String>(\"topic\", 10)`: the message type goes inside `< >`, then the topic name and the queue size.",
        "`auto timer_callback = [this]() -> void { ... };` is a **lambda**: a small function without a name, written right where it is needed.",
        "`create_wall_timer(500ms, timer_callback)` calls the lambda every 500 milliseconds, like `create_timer(0.5, self.timer_callback)`.",
        "`RCLCPP_INFO(...)` prints a log message, like `self.get_logger().info(...)`.",
        "The `SharedPtr` members hold the timer and the publisher. They are smart pointers that clean up by themselves.",
        "`main()` starts ROS 2 (`rclcpp::init`), builds the node and keeps it running (`rclcpp::spin`), then stops (`rclcpp::shutdown`)."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "words",
       "label": "Two new C++ words",
       "items": [
        [
         "std::make_shared<T>()",
         "builds an object of type T and gives back a smart pointer to it"
        ],
        [
         "[this]() { ... }",
         "a lambda: a tiny function without a name; `[this]` lets it use the node's members"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "twin",
       "left": {
        "label": "Python callback",
        "code": "def timer_callback(self):\n    msg = String()\n    msg.data = f'Hello, world! {self.i}'\n    self.publisher_.publish(msg)\n    self.i += 1",
        "lang": "python"
       },
       "right": {
        "label": "C++ lambda",
        "code": "auto timer_callback = [this]() -> void {\n  auto message = std_msgs::msg::String();\n  message.data = \"Hello, world! \" + std::to_string(this->count_++);\n  this->publisher_->publish(message);\n};",
        "lang": "cpp"
       }
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Which C++ line does the job of Python's `super().__init__('minimal_publisher')`?",
       "options": [
        "MinimalPublisher() : Node(\"minimal_publisher\"), count_(0)",
        "rclcpp::init(argc, argv);",
        "auto message = std_msgs::msg::String();",
        "rclcpp::shutdown();"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "The constructor's initialiser list calls the parent `Node(...)` with the node's name.",
       "hint": "Look for the node's name."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "In `create_wall_timer(500ms, timer_callback)`, how often is the callback called?",
       "options": [
        "Twice per second",
        "Every 500 seconds",
        "Only once",
        "500 times per second"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "500 ms is 0.5 seconds, so twice per second.",
       "hint": "1000 ms make one second."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "What is `[this]() -> void { ... }`?",
       "options": [
        "A lambda: a small function without a name",
        "A Python list",
        "A class",
        "A topic name"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Lambdas let you write a callback right where you need it.",
       "hint": "It is a kind of function."
      },
      {
       "t": "fill",
       "q": "Which rclcpp function keeps the node running, like `rclpy.spin`? Type it with its namespace.",
       "accept": [
        "rclcpp::spin",
        "rclcpp::spin()"
       ],
       "why": "`rclcpp::spin(...)` keeps calling the node's callbacks until you press Ctrl+C.",
       "hint": "rclcpp:: followed by the same word as in Python."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "An rclcpp node is a class that inherits from `rclcpp::Node`.",
        "The constructor names the node and creates the publisher and timer.",
        "Callbacks are often lambdas: `[this]() { ... }`.",
        "`main()`: init, spin, shutdown."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 9,
     "dayTitle": "C++ classes and ROS 2 C++ nodes"
    },
    {
     "id": "R1",
     "title": "Get Ubuntu 24.04 ready",
     "minutes": 20,
     "summary": "Choose WSL2, dual boot, a virtual machine or the college lab, and check your computer.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "Today Chiku's brain moves from the practice terminal to a real computer. ROS 2 Jazzy runs on **Ubuntu 24.04**, so first you need Ubuntu."
      },
      {
       "t": "goal",
       "items": [
        "Choose how to get Ubuntu 24.04",
        "Check that your computer is ready",
        "Update Ubuntu before installing ROS 2"
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "label": "Start early",
       "text": "Read this lesson a few days before Day 10. Downloads and restarts take time, and it is easier to ask for help early."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Four ways to get Ubuntu 24.04"
      },
      {
       "t": "list",
       "items": [
        "**WSL2 on Windows 11 (recommended for most students):** Ubuntu runs inside Windows. No partitioning, and graphical apps such as RViz work. Open PowerShell as administrator, type the command below, restart, then open Ubuntu from the Start menu.",
        "**Dual boot:** Ubuntu installed next to Windows. Fastest, exactly like the lab, but partitioning can lose data if done wrong. Do it only with a backup and with help.",
        "**Virtual machine (VMware Workstation Player or VirtualBox):** safe and easy to reset, but slower, and 3D tools like Gazebo and RViz can misbehave (especially in VirtualBox).",
        "**College lab PCs:** already set up. Use them if your laptop is old or has less than 8 GB of memory."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "wsl --install -d Ubuntu-24.04",
       "copy": true
      },
      {
       "t": "checklist",
       "title": "Is my computer ready?",
       "items": [
        "I checked my memory (RAM): **8 GB or more** is best. If I have less, I will use the lab PCs for Gazebo.",
        "I have about **50 GB of free disk space**.",
        "I checked that **virtualization is enabled** (Task Manager, Performance, CPU shows *Virtualization: Enabled*).",
        "I have a good internet connection for about **3 to 5 GB** of downloads, and my charger is plugged in."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "After Ubuntu starts"
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "lsb_release -a                       # should say Ubuntu 24.04\nsudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y",
       "copy": true
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "Ubuntu asks for **your** password whenever you use `sudo`. Nothing appears while you type it. That is normal: type it and press Enter."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Which Ubuntu version does ROS 2 Jazzy need?",
       "options": [
        "Ubuntu 24.04 (Noble)",
        "Ubuntu 22.04",
        "Ubuntu 20.04",
        "Any version"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Jazzy's ready-made packages are built for Ubuntu 24.04.",
       "hint": "Look at the lesson title."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Your laptop has 4 GB of RAM. What is the best plan for the Gazebo lessons?",
       "options": [
        "Use the college lab PCs for the simulations",
        "Run three virtual machines",
        "Skip Day 10",
        "Turn off the internet"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "Gazebo needs more memory; the lab PCs are ready for it.",
       "hint": "Which option is realistic?"
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "ROS 2 Jazzy needs Ubuntu 24.04.",
        "WSL2 on Windows 11 is the easiest start; the lab PCs are always an option.",
        "Update Ubuntu before installing ROS 2."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 10,
     "dayTitle": "Install ROS 2 and set up the lab"
    },
    {
     "id": "R2",
     "title": "Install ROS 2 Jazzy",
     "minutes": 45,
     "summary": "Rehearse the official installation in the practice terminal, then install on your own Ubuntu.",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "story",
       "text": "This is the big moment: installing ROS 2 Jazzy. First you do a full **dress rehearsal** in the practice terminal, so the real installation has no surprises."
      },
      {
       "t": "goal",
       "items": [
        "Follow the official ROS 2 Jazzy installation steps",
        "Understand what each step does",
        "Test the installation with a talker and a listener"
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "The official steps, explained"
      },
      {
       "t": "steps",
       "items": [
        "**Language settings (locale):** make sure Ubuntu uses UTF-8 text.",
        "**Universe:** switch on Ubuntu's community software collection, which ROS 2 needs.",
        "**ROS 2 package source:** install a small package called `ros2-apt-source`. It tells apt where ROS 2 packages live and adds their security key.",
        "**apt update:** refresh the catalogue so apt can see the ROS 2 packages.",
        "**Install:** `ros-dev-tools` (build tools) and `ros-jazzy-desktop` (ROS 2 with RViz, rqt and demos).",
        "**Source:** load ROS 2 into the terminal, and add that line to `~/.bashrc`.",
        "**Test:** a talker in one terminal and a listener in another."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "Two of the commands are very long. In the rehearsal you may copy them with the **Copy** buttons and paste them into the practice terminal (Ctrl+V)."
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "export ROS_APT_SOURCE_VERSION=$(curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/ros-infrastructure/ros-apt-source/releases/latest | grep -F \"tag_name\" | awk -F\\\" '{print $4}')",
       "copy": true
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "curl -L -o /tmp/ros2-apt-source.deb \"https://github.com/ros-infrastructure/ros-apt-source/releases/download/${ROS_APT_SOURCE_VERSION}/ros2-apt-source_${ROS_APT_SOURCE_VERSION}.$(. /etc/os-release && echo ${UBUNTU_CODENAME:-${VERSION_CODENAME}})_all.deb\"",
       "copy": true
      },
      {
       "t": "term",
       "title": "Dress rehearsal: install ROS 2 Jazzy on a fresh Ubuntu",
       "fresh": true,
       "allowPaste": true,
       "intro": "This practice terminal is a brand-new Ubuntu 24.04 without ROS 2. Follow the steps. Mistakes are safe here!",
       "tasks": [
        {
         "do": "Check the language settings: `locale`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^locale$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: locale",
         "sol": "locale"
        },
        {
         "do": "Install the locale tools: `sudo apt update && sudo apt install locales`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "install locales"
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo apt update && sudo apt install locales",
         "sol": "sudo apt update && sudo apt install locales"
        },
        {
         "do": "Generate UTF-8: `sudo locale-gen en_US en_US.UTF-8`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^sudo locale-gen en_US en_US\\.UTF-8$"
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo locale-gen en_US en_US.UTF-8",
         "sol": "sudo locale-gen en_US en_US.UTF-8"
        },
        {
         "do": "Make it the default: `sudo update-locale LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 LANG=en_US.UTF-8`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^sudo update-locale LC_ALL=en_US\\.UTF-8 LANG=en_US\\.UTF-8$"
         },
         "hint": "Type it exactly, with sudo at the start.",
         "sol": "sudo update-locale LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 LANG=en_US.UTF-8"
        },
        {
         "do": "Use it in this terminal: `export LANG=en_US.UTF-8`",
         "check": {
          "env": {
           "LANG": "en_US.UTF-8"
          }
         },
         "hint": "Type: export LANG=en_US.UTF-8",
         "sol": "export LANG=en_US.UTF-8"
        },
        {
         "do": "Install the repository tool: `sudo apt install software-properties-common`",
         "check": {
          "hasCmd": "add-apt-repository"
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo apt install software-properties-common",
         "sol": "sudo apt install software-properties-common"
        },
        {
         "do": "Switch on universe: `sudo add-apt-repository universe`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^sudo add-apt-repository universe$",
          "ok": true
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo add-apt-repository universe",
         "sol": "sudo add-apt-repository universe"
        },
        {
         "do": "Install curl: `sudo apt update && sudo apt install curl -y`",
         "check": {
          "hasCmd": "curl"
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo apt update && sudo apt install curl -y",
         "sol": "sudo apt update && sudo apt install curl -y"
        },
        {
         "do": "Look up the newest ros2-apt-source version: copy the **first** long command above and paste it here.",
         "check": {
          "env": {
           "ROS_APT_SOURCE_VERSION": "1.1.0"
          }
         },
         "hint": "Click Copy on the first long command box, click in the terminal, press Ctrl+V, then Enter.",
         "sol": "export ROS_APT_SOURCE_VERSION=$(curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/ros-infrastructure/ros-apt-source/releases/latest | grep -F \"tag_name\" | awk -F\\\" '{print $4}')"
        },
        {
         "do": "Download the package: copy the **second** long command and paste it here.",
         "check": {
          "file": "/tmp/ros2-apt-source.deb"
         },
         "hint": "Click Copy on the second long command box, paste with Ctrl+V, press Enter.",
         "sol": "curl -L -o /tmp/ros2-apt-source.deb \"https://github.com/ros-infrastructure/ros-apt-source/releases/download/${ROS_APT_SOURCE_VERSION}/ros2-apt-source_${ROS_APT_SOURCE_VERSION}.$(. /etc/os-release && echo ${UBUNTU_CODENAME:-${VERSION_CODENAME}})_all.deb\""
        },
        {
         "do": "Install it: `sudo dpkg -i /tmp/ros2-apt-source.deb`",
         "check": {
          "rosRepo": true
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo dpkg -i /tmp/ros2-apt-source.deb",
         "sol": "sudo dpkg -i /tmp/ros2-apt-source.deb"
        },
        {
         "do": "Refresh, then install the developer tools: `sudo apt update && sudo apt install ros-dev-tools`",
         "check": {
          "hasCmd": "colcon"
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo apt update && sudo apt install ros-dev-tools",
         "sol": "sudo apt update && sudo apt install ros-dev-tools"
        },
        {
         "do": "Install ROS 2 Jazzy Desktop: `sudo apt install ros-jazzy-desktop`",
         "check": {
          "rosInstalled": true
         },
         "hint": "Type: sudo apt install ros-jazzy-desktop",
         "sol": "sudo apt install ros-jazzy-desktop"
        },
        {
         "do": "Load ROS 2: `source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash`",
         "check": {
          "sourced": true
         },
         "hint": "Type: source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash",
         "sol": "source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash"
        },
        {
         "do": "Test it: `ros2 run demo_nodes_cpp talker`",
         "check": {
          "cmd": "^ros2 run demo_nodes_cpp talker$",
          "outputHas": "Hello World"
         },
         "hint": "Type: ros2 run demo_nodes_cpp talker",
         "sol": "ros2 run demo_nodes_cpp talker"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Now on your real computer"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "text": "When the rehearsal works, open the terminal on your real Ubuntu 24.04 and run the same commands, one by one. The **Copy** button copies everything below; you can also copy and paste one line at a time."
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "bash",
       "text": "# 1. Language settings (UTF-8)\nlocale\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install locales\nsudo locale-gen en_US en_US.UTF-8\nsudo update-locale LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 LANG=en_US.UTF-8\nexport LANG=en_US.UTF-8\n\n# 2. Ubuntu universe repository\nsudo apt install software-properties-common\nsudo add-apt-repository universe\n\n# 3. ROS 2 package source (ros2-apt-source)\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install curl -y\nexport ROS_APT_SOURCE_VERSION=$(curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/ros-infrastructure/ros-apt-source/releases/latest | grep -F \"tag_name\" | awk -F\\\" '{print $4}')\ncurl -L -o /tmp/ros2-apt-source.deb \"https://github.com/ros-infrastructure/ros-apt-source/releases/download/${ROS_APT_SOURCE_VERSION}/ros2-apt-source_${ROS_APT_SOURCE_VERSION}.$(. /etc/os-release && echo ${UBUNTU_CODENAME:-${VERSION_CODENAME}})_all.deb\"\nsudo dpkg -i /tmp/ros2-apt-source.deb\n\n# 4. Install ROS 2 Jazzy\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install ros-dev-tools\nsudo apt upgrade\nsudo apt install ros-jazzy-desktop\n\n# 5. Load ROS 2 now and in every new terminal\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\necho \"source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\" >> ~/.bashrc",
       "copy": true
      },
      {
       "t": "tip",
       "text": "The big install downloads a few gigabytes and can take 10 to 40 minutes. Keep your laptop plugged in, and don't close the terminal until you see the prompt again."
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "Test with two terminals"
      },
      {
       "t": "steps",
       "items": [
        "Terminal 1: `ros2 run demo_nodes_cpp talker`",
        "Open a second terminal (Ctrl+Alt+T): `ros2 run demo_nodes_py listener`",
        "You should see `I heard: [Hello World: 1]`, then 2, 3 and so on. Press Ctrl+C in both terminals to stop."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "text": "If something goes wrong"
      },
      {
       "t": "list",
       "items": [
        "**NO_PUBKEY or 'not signed' errors:** an old setup is in the way. Run `sudo rm /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ros2.list`, repeat step 3, then `sudo apt update`.",
        "**Unmet dependencies:** run `sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade` first, then install again.",
        "**Could not get lock /var/lib/dpkg/lock-frontend:** Ubuntu is updating in the background. Wait 10 minutes or restart. Never delete lock files.",
        "**ros2: command not found:** run `source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash`, and check that the line is in `~/.bashrc`.",
        "**The listener hears nothing:** both terminals need the same `ROS_DOMAIN_ID` (see the next lesson)."
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "What does the `ros2-apt-source` package do?",
       "options": [
        "Tells apt where to find ROS 2 packages and adds their security key",
        "Installs all of ROS 2",
        "Starts the talker",
        "Upgrades Ubuntu to a new version"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "It only sets up the package source; the actual install comes after `apt update`.",
       "hint": "It is about where packages come from."
      },
      {
       "t": "mcq",
       "q": "Right after installing ros2-apt-source, `sudo apt install ros-jazzy-desktop` says `Unable to locate package`. What did you forget?",
       "options": [
        "sudo apt update",
        "Restarting the computer",
        "source ~/.bashrc",
        "sudo rosdep init"
       ],
       "answer": 0,
       "why": "apt must refresh its catalogue before it can see the new ROS 2 packages.",
       "hint": "The catalogue is old."
      },
      {
       "t": "order",
       "q": "Put the main installation steps in order.",
       "items": [
        "Set up the UTF-8 locale",
        "Enable the universe repository",
        "Install ros2-apt-source",
        "sudo apt update",
        "sudo apt install ros-jazzy-desktop",
        "source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash"
       ],
       "why": "Prepare Ubuntu, add the ROS 2 package source, refresh, install, then load ROS 2.",
       "hint": "Preparation comes first; loading ROS 2 comes last."
      },
      {
       "t": "recap",
       "items": [
        "The official order: locale, universe, ros2-apt-source, update, install, source.",
        "Run `sudo apt update` after adding a new package source.",
        "Test with talker and listener in two terminals."
       ]
      }
     ],
     "day": 10,
     "dayTitle": "Install ROS 2 and set up the lab"
